abstract: >
  We study the joint determination of parallel trade policy and trade barriers in a North–South model of international trade. Income differences generate differences in willingness to pay across countries and thereby create opportunities for international arbitrage. When parallel trade is allowed and income differences are sufficiently large, some Northern firms refrain from exporting to the South to avoid arbitrage, allowing them to charge higher prices at home. Restricting parallel trade induces more Northern firms to export, leading to a pro-competitive effect on prices in the South, and shifts the terms of trade in favour of the poor country. Consequently, the South prefers to prohibit parallel trade, whereas the North prefers to allow it. Allowing parallel trade also overturns standard trade-policy incentives: the North prefers free trade, while the South prefers higher trade barriers because they reduce the threat of arbitrage, inducing more exports from the North. When parallel trade is prohibited, both countries prefer free trade. The global welfare-maximising policy combines a ban on parallel trade with free trade.
